\section{Problem Definition, Scope and Objectives of the Project}

\subsection{Problem Definition}

In most higher educational institutions, peer mentorship occurs through informal and unstructured channels such as personal messaging applications, social media groups, or casual academic interactions. While these platforms enable communication, they lack a structured framework for mentorship discovery, request management, and interaction tracking.

A significant challenge within existing informal systems is limited accessibility. Students who are introverted, socially hesitant, or lacking strong peer networks often struggle to identify and approach suitable mentors. The absence of a transparent mentor discovery mechanism results in unequal access to academic guidance and missed opportunities for structured support.

Furthermore, current communication channels do not provide a centralized platform for academic discussion and domain-based collaboration. Discussions are fragmented across multiple platforms, leading to repetitive queries, scattered knowledge, and the loss of valuable academic insights.

Another limitation is the absence of structured interaction logging. Mentorship sessions and discussions are not recorded in a systematic format, preventing analysis of engagement patterns, subject demand, or mentorship effectiveness. Without structured data collection, student communities are unable to leverage data-driven approaches for improving academic collaboration.

Additionally, there is no intelligent system to recommend suitable mentors based on skills, interests, or domain compatibility. Students often rely on personal familiarity rather than expertise alignment when selecting mentors, resulting in suboptimal mentorship relationships.

Therefore, there is a need for a centralized, structured, and intelligent mentorship and academic networking platform that enables transparent mentor discovery, organized communication, community-driven discussions, and structured interaction logging within an institute-exclusive environment.

\subsection{Scope of the Project}

The scope of MentorLink is confined to the development of an institute-exclusive web-based mentorship and academic networking platform. The system focuses on formalizing peer mentorship and enabling structured academic collaboration among students.

The project includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Secure institute-based user authentication.
    \item Structured student profile and academic portfolio management.
    \item Mentor discovery and recommendation mechanisms.
    \item Mentorship request and lifecycle management.
    \item One-to-one messaging and group communication.
    \item Public and private academic communities.
    \item Structured interaction logging for mentorship sessions.
\end{itemize}

The system is designed with a scalable backend architecture to support future integration of advanced data mining and analytics techniques. However, large-scale predictive analytics and machine learning models will be implemented in subsequent phases of the project.

\subsection{Objectives}

\begin{itemize}
    \item To develop a secure full-stack mentorship and social networking platform (MentorLink) with core features such as user profiles, posts, communities, group chat, and follow relationships.
    
    \item To enable smooth mentor–student interaction through personalized profiles, discussion feeds, communities, and real-time group messaging.
    
    \item To mine user-interaction data (posts, likes, follows, communities, messages) for identifying behavioural patterns and engagement trends.
    
    \item To design recommendation mechanisms that suggest relevant mentors, peers, communities, and content based on mined user behaviour.
\end{itemize}

