\section{Literature Survey}

\subsection{Scope and Methodology}

This literature survey examines research related to Educational Data Mining (EDM), peer mentoring effectiveness, personalized recommendation systems, and machine learning-based mentor–mentee matching frameworks. Relevant studies were identified from academic journals, conference proceedings, and recent survey papers published between 2023 and 2024. The selected works were chosen based on methodological rigor, empirical validation, statistical significance, and relevance to academic mentorship and recommendation platforms. 

The reviewed literature provides a theoretical and empirical foundation for MentorLink’s architecture, particularly in areas such as structured interaction logging, behavioural analytics, recommendation mechanisms, and mentoring effectiveness. The survey also highlights recurring challenges such as cold-start problems, data privacy concerns, ethical AI use, and scalability constraints in educational systems. These findings guide the design of MentorLink as a data-driven, scalable, and analytics-ready mentorship platform.

\subsection{Paper Summaries}

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Papadogiannis, Wallace \& Karountzou (2024).} \textit{Educational Data Mining (EDM): Trends and Applications.}\\
Provides a comprehensive synthesis of the evolution, significance, methods, and applications of Educational Data Mining. The study reports exponential growth in EDM research using the model $y = 4 \times 10^{-143} e^{0.1646x}$ ($R^2 = 0.89$), indicating strong upward research trends. The authors categorize EDM data into three hierarchical levels: Micro (individual learner interactions), Meso (course-level data), and Macro (institution-level data). Academic performance data (36.2\%) is the most widely used category, followed by behavioural and programming data (20.3\% each). Common techniques include classification, regression, clustering, association mining, and NLP. The study supports MentorLink’s structured interaction logging approach and its analytics-ready backend design.

\item \textbf{Gehreke, Schilling \& Kauffeld (2024).} \textit{Effectiveness of Peer Mentoring in Higher Education.}\\
Conducted a meta-analysis of 23 empirical studies focusing on peer mentoring during study entry phases. The findings indicate that structured peer mentoring improves academic performance by approximately 12\% in grades and completion rates. Key success factors include structured program design, mentor training, regular monitoring, and institutional commitment. The study emphasizes that technology-mediated mentoring platforms can scale effectively when supported by proper pedagogical design. These findings justify MentorLink’s structured mentorship lifecycle model.

\item \textbf{Bin, Zuhairi \& Morcos (2024).} \textit{Personalized Learning Recommendation Systems.}\\
Surveyed 87 research studies on personalized recommendation systems in educational contexts. The study found that hybrid recommendation approaches (collaborative + content-based + deep learning) achieve 85–92\% accuracy compared to 65–75\% for single-method systems. The cold-start problem was identified as a major challenge, recommending explicit preference collection during onboarding. Context-aware recommendation systems improved learner satisfaction by 23\%, while Explainable AI increased user trust by 34\%. This research supports MentorLink’s future hybrid recommendation engine design.

\item \textbf{Varghese \& Mohaghegh (2023).} \textit{ML-Based Mentor–Mentee Matching.}\\
Proposed a hybrid machine learning model combining Big Five personality traits with collaborative and content-based filtering for mentor–mentee matching. The study achieved 78\% accuracy using profile data only and 89\% accuracy when interaction data was included. Personality traits such as extraversion and conscientiousness showed strong correlation with mentoring satisfaction ($r = 0.67$, $p < 0.01$). The hybrid model effectively addressed the cold-start problem through initial personality-based matching. This work provides empirical validation for structured data collection in MentorLink.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Comparative Summary}

